#{[17592186440634 :question/worked-solution "The cross-product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the cross-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(3\\,i-5\\,j+2\\,k)\\times (7\\,i-4\\,j-k) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & -4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 7 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 7 & -4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(5+8)i-(-3-14)j+(-12+35)k \\\\  & =13\\,i+17\\,j+23\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186440736 :answer/value "-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 10 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440737 :answer/value "-2\\begin{vmatrix}10 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -9 & 10 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440738 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 13 & -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440739 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 13 & 1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440740 :answer/value "3\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 8 & -11 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 6 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440741 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -7 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440742 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 13 & -5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440743 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 11 & -10 \\\\ 1 & -7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -14 & 19\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440744 :answer/value "-3\\begin{vmatrix}11 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -9 & 10 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440745 :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -8 & -10 & 4\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440747 :answer/value "-3\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 8 & -11 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440749 :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}10 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -9 & 11 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440752 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 7 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 13 & -5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440753 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}13 & -8 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 7 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 12\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440754 :answer/value "-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -8 & -10 & 4\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440755 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 3 & -11 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 9 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440756 :answer/value "-3\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 4\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440757 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 5 & -8\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440761 :answer/value "4\\begin{vmatrix}10 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 11 & -6 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -7\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440762 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}13 & 10 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -16 & -21\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440764 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}13 & 11 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -16 & 21\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440765 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}13 & 11 & -10 \\\\ 10 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -13 & 24\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440766 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}10 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 11 & -9 \\\\ 8 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440767 :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 8 & -11 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440768 :answer/value "-2\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 8 & -10 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186440776 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -3 & 13 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 19 & 2 & -7 & -5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{32}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186440776 :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries above and below ${a}_{32}=1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -3 & 13 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 19 & 2 & -7 & -5\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 13 & 0 & -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 13 & 0 & -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 13 & -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 13 & -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186440777 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 6 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -8 & 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{24}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186440777 :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries above and below ${a}_{24}=2$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 6 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -8 & 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}10 & -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -9 & 11 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}10 & -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -9 & 11 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 3- & 7 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+4}\\begin{vmatrix}10 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -9 & 11 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}10 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -9 & 11 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186440778 :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -9 & 5\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of ${a}_{11}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*"] [17592186440778 :question/worked-solution "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the right of ${a}_{11}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -9 & 5\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+(-2){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+2{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -8 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $1$st row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -8 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -8 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -8 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 9 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(3-45) \\\\  & =126.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186440779 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -6 & -11 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{22}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186440779 :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries above and below ${a}_{22}=2$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -6 & -11 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 0 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & -11 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 6 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 0 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & -11 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 6 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 8 & -11 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 8 & -11 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186440780 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 & 12 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 6 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -8 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 5 & -6\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of ${a}_{21}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186440780 :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the right of ${a}_{21}=2$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 & 12 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 6 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -8 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 5 & -6\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+(-2){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+2{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -8 & -10 & 4\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -8 & -10 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -8 & -10 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -8 & -10 & 4\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186440781 :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -12 & -2 & -7 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 12 & 7 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of ${a}_{21}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*"] [17592186440781 :question/worked-solution "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the right of ${a}_{21}=2$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -12 & -2 & -7 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 12 & 7 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+3{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+2{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (3)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6(-24+25) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186440782 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -7 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 & -5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above ${a}_{42}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186440782 :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries above ${a}_{42}=1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -7 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 & -5\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{4} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{4} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}13 & 0 & 11 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -16 & 21 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 & -5\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}13 & 0 & 11 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -16 & 21 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}\\begin{vmatrix}13 & 11 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -16 & 21\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}13 & 11 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -16 & 21\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186441125 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186441130 :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}\\,\\,\\,3 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 10 & \\,\\,\\,5\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,5\\end{vmatrix}}\\end{aligned}"] [17592186441133 :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}\\,\\,\\,2 & \\,\\,-3 \\\\ 15 & \\,\\,\\,1\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,5\\end{vmatrix}}\\end{aligned}"] [17592186441135 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186441136 :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}\\,\\,\\,2 & \\,\\,-3 \\\\ 10 & \\,\\,\\,5\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,5\\end{vmatrix}}\\end{aligned}"] [17592186441137 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186441138 :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\,\\,-3 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,5\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 10 & \\,\\,\\,5\\end{vmatrix}}\\end{aligned}"] [17592186441140 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186441142 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186441143 :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}\\,\\,\\,2 & \\,\\,-3 \\\\ 10 & \\,\\,\\,5\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}}\\end{aligned}"] [17592186441147 :question/problem "Let $⟨2,y⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+ky &=5 \\\\ x+2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}4 & k \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=\\begin{vmatrix}5 & k \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & k \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=\\begin{vmatrix}5 & k \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}4 & k \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=2\\begin{vmatrix}5 & k \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186441147 :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & k \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & k \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}5 & k \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}4 & k \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\ 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & k \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & k \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [17592186441148 :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y &=2 \\\\ 2x+3y &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we construct ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, according to Cramer's rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186441149 :question/problem "Let $⟨-12,y⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7x+9y &=42 \\\\ kx+10y &=-4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ k & 10\\end{vmatrix}=-12\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ k & 10\\end{vmatrix}=-38\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ k & 10\\end{vmatrix}=456\n$$"] [17592186441149 :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ k & 10\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =\\begin{vmatrix}42 & 9 \\\\ -4 & 10\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =420+36 \\\\  & =456.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D} \\\\ -12 & =\\frac{456}{\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ k & 10\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ k & 10\\end{vmatrix} & =\\frac{456}{-12} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ k & 10\\end{vmatrix} & =-38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [17592186444325 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4t}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4t+1}{4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4t+1}}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\right)}{{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{4t+1}}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\right)}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{(4t+1)^{3}}}{8\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(4t+1)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186444327 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩=⟨8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{100{cos}^{2}⁡2t}=10|\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),-6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩=⟨-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,0⟩\\times ⟨-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & 0 \\\\ -16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & 12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ -16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & 12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =(96\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-96\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{0}{(10|\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t|)^{3}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186444328 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6-3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-2)⟩=⟨-6t,\\,0,\\,6⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-6t)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36({t}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative ${r}^{″}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-6t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6)⟩=⟨-6,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨-6t,0,6⟩\\times ⟨-6,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -6t & 0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-6t & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-6t & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =-36\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{36}{{(6\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1})}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{6({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186444329 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(0,0,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\sqrt{3}t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{3}⟩ & =⟨0,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}t &=0 \\\\ {t}^{2} &=0 \\\\ {t}^{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(0,0,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,2t,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,2(0),\\,3({0}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{3},0,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+0+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,6t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,6(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186444331 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2cos)^{2}+(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩=⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =(-6{cos}^{2}⁡t-6{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-6\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186444332 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,1,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{2t},\\,2t⟩ & =⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} &=1 \\\\ {e}^{2t} &=1 \\\\ 2t &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{2t},\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,2,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{2t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,4{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨1,4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =-8i+2j+2k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{{3}^{3}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186444333 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(\\frac{2}{3},1,1)$ on the given curve. We know that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},1,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3},{t}^{2},\\,\\,t⟩ & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3} &=\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {t}^{2} &=1 \\\\ t &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(\\frac{2}{3},1,1)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(1),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},\\,2t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨2,2,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4t,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨4,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =-2i+4j-4k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{3}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186444457 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & \\pi  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186444458 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186444470 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & \\pi  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186444473 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186444476 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186444478 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186444493 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186444498 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186444695 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(2),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(2)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(2) & =⟨1,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(2) & =⟨0,-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(2)$ and ${r}^{″}(2)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(2)\\times {r}^{″}(2) & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}}\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(2)\\times {r}^{″}(2)‖ & =\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(2) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(2){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(2)\\times {r}^{″}(2)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\left(\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\right)}^{3}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{27}{16\\sqrt{2}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186444697 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ and $‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ and ${r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\times {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨0,-4,0⟩\\times ⟨-4,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & \\,\\,-4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =-16\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\times {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖ & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\times {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖}=\\frac{{4}^{3}}{\\,16\\,}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186459212 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(5j)-(3i+1j+2k) \\\\  & =-3i+4j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,4,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(3i+3j+1k)-(3i+1j+2k) \\\\  & =2j-1k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(1i+4j+2k)-(3i+1j+2k) \\\\  & =-2i+3j \\\\  & =⟨-2,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(2\\cdot 0-(-1)(3))-4(0\\cdot 0-(-1)(-2))+(-2)(0\\cdot 3-2\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =-3(3)-4(-2)-2(4) \\\\  & =-9-8+8 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-9|=9.\n$$"] [17592186459217 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i+3j+4k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =i+2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨1,2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-i+2j)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =-2i+j-k \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(3i+5k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =2i-j+4k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(1\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot (-1))-2((-2)\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot 2)+3((-2)\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1(4-1)-2(-8+2)+3(2-2) \\\\  & =3+12+0 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|15|=15.\n$$"] [17592186459221 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k & 1 \\\\ -8 & 3 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & 1 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -8 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & k \\\\ -8 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5(k\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot 1)+2(3\\cdot (-4)-(-8)\\cdot 1)+2(3\\cdot 3-(-8)\\cdot k) \\\\  & =-20k-15-8+18+16k \\\\  & =-4k-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =0 \\\\ -4k-5 & =0 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{5}{4}.$"] [17592186459239 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(8i-3j+3k)-(8i+5k) \\\\  & =-3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨0,-3,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(5i+2j+5k)-(8i+5k) \\\\  & =-3i+2j \\\\  & =⟨-3,2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(7i-j+4k)-(8i+5k) \\\\  & =-i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0+3((-3)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 0)-2((-3)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =9-10 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-1|=1.\n$$"] [17592186459261 :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-((-4)\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 2)-2(1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 2)+5(1\\cdot 5-0\\cdot (-4)) \\\\  & =14-2+25 \\\\  & =37\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|37|=37.\n$$"] [17592186459264 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 9 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 4 & t & t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ t & t\\end{vmatrix}-9\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & t\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ 4 & t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =9(6\\cdot t-t\\cdot 0)-9(5\\cdot t-4\\cdot 0)-2(5\\cdot t-4\\cdot 6) \\\\  & =54t-45t-10t+48 \\\\  & =48-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =0 \\\\ 48-t & =0 \\\\ t & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=48.$"] [17592186459275 :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -7 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(5\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-7))-2(3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-7))+4(3\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =10-26-20 \\\\  & =-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|-36|=36.\n$$"] [17592186459280 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i+8j+2k)-(9j+4k) \\\\  & =3i-j-2k \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+9j+k)-(9j+4k) \\\\  & =2i-3k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(-i+7j+7k)-(9j+4k) \\\\  & =-i-2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(0\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot (-3))+(2\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot (-3))-2(2\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-18+3+8 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-7|=7.\n$$"] [17592186459283 :question/worked-solution "The volume of a parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(3\\cdot 5-0\\cdot 2)+0+2(1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =60+0+4 \\\\  & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|64|=64.\n$$"] [17592186459292 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(4i+3j+2k)-(3i-2j+2k) \\\\  & =i+5j \\\\  & =⟨1,5,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(i+3j+5k)-(3i-2j+2k) \\\\  & =-2i+5j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-2,5,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(2i+4j-6k)-(3i-2j+2k) \\\\  & =-i+6j-8k \\\\  & =⟨-1,6,-8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 6 & -8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -8\\end{vmatrix}-5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -8\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(5\\cdot (-8)-6\\cdot 3)-5((-2)\\cdot (-8)-(-1)\\cdot 3)+0 \\\\  & =-58-95 \\\\  & =-153.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-153|=153.\n$$"] [17592186459306 :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-((-2)\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 1)-((-2)\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =5+5 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|10|=10.\n$$"] [17592186459308 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}12 & -2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -9 & 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-(-2x)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -9 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =12(5\\cdot 8-4\\cdot (-2))+2x(1\\cdot 8-(-9)\\cdot (-2))+(1\\cdot 4-5\\cdot (-9)) \\\\  & =576-20x+49 \\\\  & =625-20x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 625-20x & =0 \\\\ 20x & =625 \\\\ x & =\\frac{125}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $x=\\frac{125}{4}.$"] [17592186475912 :question/worked-solution "From the equation of a plane in the parametric form $r=p+tv+sw$, we have that its normal vector is $n=v\\times w.$ In our case, the normal vectors to $Π$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =⟨1,0,2⟩\\times ⟨0,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =4i-3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨4,-3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane $Π$ in dot product form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,-3,-2⟩ & =⟨5,3,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨4,-3,-2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,-3,-2⟩ & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨1,-1,2⟩+w⟨3,4,-1⟩$ into the equation of the plane in dot product form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨4,-3,-2⟩ & =19 \\\\ ⟨1+3w,-1+4w,2-w⟩\\cdot ⟨4,-3,-2⟩ & =19 \\\\ 4(1+3w)-3(-1+4w)-2(2-w) & =19 \\\\ 4+12w+3-12w-4+2w & =19 \\\\ 2w & =16 \\\\ w & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $q$ of the intersection point $Q,$ we substitute $w=8$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q & =⟨1+3(8),-1+4(8),2-8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨25,31,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $Q(25,31,-6).$"] [17592186475975 :question/worked-solution "From the equation of a plane in the parametric form $r=p+tv+sw$, we have that its normal vector is $n=v\\times w.$ In our case, the normal vectors to $Π$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =⟨2,0,2⟩\\times ⟨1,4,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =-8i+2j+8k \\\\  & =⟨-8,2,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane $Π$ in dot product form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-8,2,8⟩ & =⟨-15,1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-8,2,8⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-8,2,8⟩ & =130.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨2,3,1⟩+w⟨-3,-2,3⟩$ into the equation of the plane in dot product form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨-8,2,8⟩ & =130 \\\\ ⟨2-3w,3-2w,1+3w⟩\\cdot ⟨-8,2,8⟩ & =130 \\\\ -8(2-3w)+2(3-2w)+8(1+3w) & =130 \\\\ -16+24w+6-4w+8+24w & =130 \\\\ 44w & =132 \\\\ w & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $q$ of the intersection point $Q,$ we substitute $w=3$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q & =⟨2-3(3),3-2(3),1+3(3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-7,-3,10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $Q(-7,-3,10).$"] [17592186478445 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(1)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\frac{1}{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(1)$ and ${r}^{″}(1)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =-\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}=\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}{\\,1\\,}=2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186481388 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ -8 & 0 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -8 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )((4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+24) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+8) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial $\\,{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$"] [17592186483607 :answer/value "-3\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186483744 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186483748 :answer/value "-3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186483825 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 4 & 7 & -8\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186483854 :answer/value "-3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186483895 :answer/value "-3\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186483937 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -5 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8 & 8\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186483994 :answer/value "-3\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 9 & 7\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186484004 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -8 & 7\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186484044 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -8 & 6\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186484204 :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -6 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 10 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{13}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*"] [17592186484204 :question/worked-solution "Using elementary row operations, let's make entries below ${a}_{13}=-2$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -6 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 10 & 1\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+5{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 0 & -7 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -11 & 0 & 11\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 0 & -7 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -11 & 0 & 11\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 10 & -7 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -11 & 11\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 10 & -7 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -11 & 11\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}10 & -7 \\\\ -11 & 11\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(110-77) \\\\  & =-66.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
